{"title":"Test ID - 65 [Round - 1 DBMS Test - 1] - 2025","fileName":"Test_ID_-_65_Round_-_1_DBMS_Test_-_1_-_2025.html","branch":"COMPUTER SCIENCE ENGINEERING","series":"GATE - 2025","provider":"GATE ZEAL","duration":7200,"questions":[{"qnum":1,"type":"MCQ","correct":"B","right":1,"wrong":0.33,"qtext":"<p><img src=\"https://cms-cdn.classplus.co/parserImages/4f45b216-7d64-4dfa-855e-4b8253fb5b0d.png\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<p>a</p>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<p>b</p>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"C","text":"<p>c</p>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<p>d</p>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p><img src=\"https://cms-cdn.classplus.co/parserImages/e04b91f1-e7d7-490f-812b-376ba8f00235.png\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","subject":"Test ID - 65 [Round - 1 DBMS Test - 1] - 2025"},{"qnum":2,"type":"NAT","correct":"4","right":1,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p><img src=\"https://cms-cdn.classplus.co/parserImages/35da7899-4812-4502-b9b6-2e047bfbf456.png\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" /><img src=\"https://cdn-wl-assets.classplus.co/production/single/vlvkc/04254bd5-3730-4db2-9fba-adc9289f1cf9.jpeg\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","options":[],"nat":{"low":"4","high":"4"},"solution":"<p><strong><span>Answer: 4<o:p></o:p></span></strong></p>\n<p><strong><span>Solution:<o:p></o:p></span></strong></p>\n<p><strong><span>There can be two types of implementations of separate chaining for hash tables<o:p></o:p></span></strong></p>\n<p><span>&middot;<span> </span></span><strong><span>Self-contained chaining</span></strong><span> or <strong>open addressing with chaining</strong> allows the first element in the chain to be stored directly in the array at that index. If further collisions occur, a linked list (or other data structure) is used to store the additional elements.<o:p></o:p></span></p>\n<p><span>&middot;<span> </span></span><strong><span>Standard separate chaining</span></strong><span> typically uses a linked list (or another dynamic structure like a tree) to handle collisions at each table index. In this case, the elements that collide (i.e., map to the same index) are placed in the list at that index.<o:p></o:p></span></p>\n<p><span>In the question <strong>Self-contained chaining </strong>as it is given in question that <strong>&ldquo;without counting the hash table element</strong>\" that means first element store in the array index and rest element at same index store at by separate chaining. <strong> </strong>If the hash table size is N, and you are inserting an element at index i, you can store the first element directly at A[i]. If there are subsequent elements that also hash to index i, these elements can be stored in a linked list or another structure that starts from A[i].<o:p></o:p></span></p>\n<p><strong><span>So, given that </span></strong><strong><span>h(x) = x mod 11</span></strong></p>\n<p><o:p></o:p></p>\n<p><span>{13, 3, 4, 22, 12, 14, 10, 34, 26, 1, 23, 43, 45, 36}, and their hash values:<o:p></o:p></span></p>\n<p><span>&middot;<span> </span></span><span>h(13)=2<o:p></o:p></span></p>\n<p><span>&middot;<span> </span></span><span>h(3)=3<o:p></o:p></span></p>\n<p><span>&middot;<span> </span></span><span>h(4)=4 <o:p></o:p></span></p>\n<p><span>&middot;<span> </span></span><span>h(22)=0 <o:p></o:p></span></p>\n<p><span>&middot;<span> </span></span><span>h(12)=1 <o:p></o:p></span></p>\n<p><span>&middot;<span> </span></span><span>h(14)=3 <o:p></o:p></span></p>\n<p><span>&middot;<span> </span></span><span>h(10)=10<o:p></o:p></span></p>\n<p><span>&middot;<span> </span></span><span>h(34)=1<o:p></o:p></span></p>\n<p><span>&middot;<span> </span></span><span>h(26)=4<o:p></o:p></span></p>\n<p><span>&middot;<span> </span></span><span>h(1)=1<o:p></o:p></span></p>\n<p><span>&middot;<span> </span></span><span>h(23)=1<o:p></o:p></span></p>\n<p><span>&middot;<span> </span></span><span>h(43)=10<o:p></o:p></span></p>\n<p><span>&middot;<span> </span></span><span>h(45)=1<o:p></o:p></span></p>\n<p><span>&middot;<span> </span></span><span>h(36)=3<o:p></o:p></span></p>\n<p><span>Now, based on this, the elements would be distributed as follows (with self-contained chaining):<o:p></o:p></span></p>\n<p><span>&middot;<span> </span></span><span>Index 0: {22}<o:p></o:p></span></p>\n<p><span>&middot;<span> </span></span><span>Index 1: {12, 34, 1, 23, 45} &rarr; Chain length = 4 (excluding the first element, 12)<o:p></o:p></span></p>\n<p><span>&middot;<span> </span></span><span>Index 2: {13}<o:p></o:p></span></p>\n<p><span>&middot;<span> </span></span><span>Index 3: {3, 14, 36} &rarr; Chain length = 2 (excluding the first element, 3)<o:p></o:p></span></p>\n<p><span>&middot;<span> </span></span><span>Index 4: {4, 26} &rarr; Chain length = 1 (excluding the first element, 4)</span></p>\n<p><span>&middot;<span> </span></span><span>Index 10: {10, 43} &rarr; Chain length = 1 (excluding the first element, 10)<o:p></o:p></span></p>\n<p>The longest chain is at index 1, where the chain length is <strong>4</strong> (excluding the first element).</p>","subject":"Test ID - 65 [Round - 1 DBMS Test - 1] - 2025"},{"qnum":3,"type":"NAT","correct":"101","right":1,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p><img src=\"https://cms-cdn.classplus.co/parserImages/d7231b03-aa01-4f24-bfc7-86cc722259ba.png\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" /><img src=\"https://cdn-wl-assets.classplus.co/production/single/vlvkc/04254bd5-3730-4db2-9fba-adc9289f1cf9.jpeg\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","options":[],"nat":{"low":"101","high":"101"},"solution":"<p><img src=\"https://cms-cdn.classplus.co/parserImages/c3adfbbf-a783-4874-b9a3-21fa2a79e8e2.png\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","subject":"Test ID - 65 [Round - 1 DBMS Test - 1] - 2025"},{"qnum":4,"type":"NAT","correct":"3","right":1,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p><img src=\"https://cms-cdn.classplus.co/parserImages/bdf9e8fb-9b24-48a8-9550-668dadba78ef.png\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" /><img src=\"https://cdn-wl-assets.classplus.co/production/single/vlvkc/04254bd5-3